Umbra-Loom

Creative Catalyst: It descends from carnival musicians who perform only during solar eclipses.

Umbra-Loom is the sound of celestial physics colliding with a decaying fairground. It replaces the traditional "warm" Nordic jazz aesthetic with cold, metallic, and mathematically precise improvisations. The ethereal qualities are not floaty; they are heavy, like the atmosphere right before a storm. The music feels like watching a ferris wheel turn in absolute silence while the sun disappears, blending deep, bone-rattling cinepads with sharp, dissonant brass and strings.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 40-70 BPM, often utilizing a "dragged" rubato feel where the drums are perpetually lagging behind the melody.
*   Harmonic Palette: Lydian-Dominant scales played in microtonal tunings; utilizes dark, crushed jazz chords (clusters) layered over sub-bass drones.
*   Production Signatures: 1) "Vacuum-Bouncing": Recordings are processed through a modified high-pass filter that only captures the air moving inside the instrument's tubing. 2) "Photophobic Compression": Gain stages that tighten when the input frequency mimics the color spectrum of a total eclipse. 3) Granular Resampling of wax cylinder recordings.
*   Dynamics: Subterranean swells; the music exists in a state of sustained tension that only resolves during the final three seconds of a track.

Scene & Origin Lore
The Umbra-Loom collective traces its lineage to a band of circus outcasts in Kiruna, Sweden, who discovered that the geomagnetic distortion during a total solar eclipse allowed their instruments to emit "ghost frequencies." They wear tailored suits fashioned from lead-lined velvet and heavy bronze eye-masks, a ritualistic aesthetic intended to shield their senses from the "daylight rot." They perform exclusively in temporary, portable theaters built from recycled telecommunications satellites, appearing in remote tundras only when the moon occults the sun.

Instruments: Modified Flügelhorn with a frozen slide, felt-dampened upright piano, decommissioned 1950s cinepad oscillation banks, and gut-string cellos tuned to a C-natural drone.

For Fans Of: Arve Henriksen, Tim Hecker, Bohren & der Club of Gore, Hatis Noit.

