Track 2: Klaxon-Fjord Soliloquy
- Story & Context: During the height of the solar black-out, a woman named Inga stood on the edge of a frozen Fjord and shouted a confession into a glass jar for the wind to carry. The air turned brittle and cold, frost spiderwebbing across her exposed skin while she wept for the years she spent listening to radio static in silence. She waited for the sun to return, but the landscape remained locked in a perpetual, muted gray, the silence becoming a physical weight on her chest. "My lungs are just empty chambers for the winter light."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A lonely, crystalline cliffside overlooking a motionless, ink-black body of water under a dim, haloed black sun, minimalist photography style.

Track 3: What Remains Of The Punch-Card?
- Story & Context: Centuries later, the ruins of the Observatory at Point Zero still vibrate with the ghost-echoes of the eclipse-musicians who vanished into the sky during the Great Occultation. Scientists excavating the site found only rusted, hole-punched cards, their jagged patterns mapping the exact frequency of the last performance. The air in the facility hums with a lingering, low-frequency pressure that feels like a forgotten memory trying to claw its way back into reality. "I am reading the scars of a song that refused to die."
